In JavaScript, you catch exceptions using a `try...catch` block. Here's the basic syntax:

## Basic Syntax

```javascript
try {
  // Code that might throw an exception
  riskyOperation();
} catch (error) {
  // Handle the exception
  console.error('An error occurred:', error.message);
}
```

## Complete Example

```javascript
try {
  const result = JSON.parse('invalid json');
  console.log(result);
} catch (error) {
  console.log('Error type:', error.name);     // "SyntaxError"
  console.log('Error message:', error.message); // "Unexpected token..."
  console.log('Full error:', error);           // The error object
}
```

## With Finally Block (optional)

The `finally` block runs regardless of whether an error occurred:

```javascript
try {
  // Risky code
  const data = JSON.parse(userInput);
} catch (error) {
  console.error('Parsing failed:', error);
} finally {
  // Always executes - good for cleanup
  console.log('Operation complete');
}
```

## Catching Specific Error Types

You can check the error type to handle different errors differently:

```javascript
try {
  // Some code that might throw different errors
} catch (error) {
  if (error instanceof TypeError) {
    console.log('Type error:', error.message);
  } else if (error instanceof ReferenceError) {
    console.log('Reference error:', error.message);
  } else {
    console.log('Unknown error:', error);
  }
}
```

## Throwing Custom Errors

You can also throw your own exceptions:

```javascript
function divide(a, b) {
  if (b === 0) {
    throw new Error('Division by zero is not allowed');
  }
  return a / b;
}

try {
  const result = divide(10, 0);
} catch (error) {
  console.log(error.message); // "Division by zero is not allowed"
}
```

## Key Points

- **`try`**: Contains code that might throw an exception
- **`catch`**: Handles the exception (the error object is passed as a parameter)
- **`finally`**: Optional block that always executes
- The error object has properties like `name`, `message`, and `stack`
- You can have multiple catch blocks by checking `instanceof` or error properties

This is the standard way to handle runtime errors in JavaScript and prevent them from crashing your application.
